1
00:01:11,071 --> 00:01:13,870
Now, hand me that thing. - This?

2
00:01:14,041 --> 00:01:15,270
No, no. LEANNE: This?

3
00:01:17,044 --> 00:01:18,444
- Dad.
- Wait.

4
00:01:18,612 --> 00:01:20,979
Dad, you have asked for
every tool known to man.

5
00:01:21,148 --> 00:01:23,628
- You'll have to be more specific.
- Hand me the thing I had...

6
00:01:23,784 --> 00:01:25,446
- This?
- No, no.

7
00:01:25,619 --> 00:01:27,281
Yeah, that, that,
yeah, give me that.

8
00:01:29,590 --> 00:01:32,219
Ow! Are we almost done?

9
00:01:32,392 --> 00:01:34,657
I have no idea.

10
00:01:34,895 --> 00:01:37,057
Dad, you know God put plumbers

11
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:38,910
- in the phone book for a reason.
- Ha, ha.

12
00:01:41,068 --> 00:01:44,004
- Ow, ow!
- Come in!

13
00:01:45,405 --> 00:01:48,068
- Dad. Dad.
- Come in.

14
00:01:48,775 --> 00:01:51,074
- Hold that. Hold that.
- Please...

15
00:01:51,311 --> 00:01:54,247
No, Dad, come right back. Oh.

16
00:01:54,414 --> 00:01:56,815
Ow. Dad.

17
00:01:57,551 --> 00:01:59,247
Ouch.

18
00:01:59,453 --> 00:02:01,149
Dad, I can't do this.

19
00:02:01,321 --> 00:02:02,414
Be right back.

20
00:02:09,963 --> 00:02:11,261
Hello, Ben.

21
00:02:13,033 --> 00:02:14,228
Laura.

22
00:02:15,469 --> 00:02:17,495
Oh, Laura.

23
00:02:17,671 --> 00:02:19,731
Aren't you grown up?

24
00:02:21,742 --> 00:02:23,870
- Well, it's been 11 years.
- Ah.

25
00:02:24,044 --> 00:02:26,104
Last time I saw you
was at Mom's funeral.

26
00:02:26,279 --> 00:02:28,839
Oh. You live in Mt. Harlan?

27
00:02:29,016 --> 00:02:32,418
No, Atlanta. I work at a clothing
store at the mall in Marietta.

28
00:02:32,586 --> 00:02:33,626
I'm the assistant manager.

29
00:02:33,787 --> 00:02:36,450
- Oh. LEANNE: Dad,
are you still out there?

30
00:02:36,623 --> 00:02:39,058
- You ever meet my daughter?
- No, I don't think I have.

31
00:02:39,226 --> 00:02:40,250
Dad?

32
00:02:41,061 --> 00:02:42,825
- Oh.
- Leanne?

33
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:44,897
The blood's
draining from my arm.

34
00:02:45,065 --> 00:02:47,193
That's okay. Turn that
loose. Come on out here.

35
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:51,065
- Oh, Dad.
- Leanne Mclntyre, this is Laura Miller.

36
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:53,368
I'm her godfather.

37
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:57,739
Oh, Laura Miller from Mt. Harlan,
yes, your parents owned the bakery.

38
00:02:57,911 --> 00:03:01,279
I live in Atlanta now. Is
everything all right with you all?

39
00:03:01,448 --> 00:03:02,973
- Yeah.
- Uh, yeah. Yeah.

40
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:05,585
Uh, Leanne was fixing the sink.

41
00:03:05,752 --> 00:03:08,620
Well, I just came by to tell
you that I'm getting married.

42
00:03:08,789 --> 00:03:11,088
Oh! Sit down here.

43
00:03:11,258 --> 00:03:12,453
Who are you marrying?

44
00:03:12,626 --> 00:03:14,652
To Paul Retzke. He
lives in Mt. Harlan too.

45
00:03:14,828 --> 00:03:17,263
Remember him?
His dad was the vet.

46
00:03:17,431 --> 00:03:18,865
Paul's a teacher.
He's wonderful.

47
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:21,832
- When's the wedding?
- In a couple of months.

48
00:03:22,002 --> 00:03:24,699
Since my parents passed
away and we both live in Atlanta,

49
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:27,340
we decided to get married
here instead of back home.

50
00:03:28,041 --> 00:03:30,408
Which brings me to why I came.

51
00:03:32,012 --> 00:03:34,880
It would mean a lot to me
if you would give me away.

52
00:03:35,048 --> 00:03:37,677
If you would rather not, I can
always ask my brother, Chuck.

53
00:03:37,851 --> 00:03:40,411
I'd be honored.

54
00:03:40,587 --> 00:03:42,647
Oh, Ben, thanks so much.

55
00:03:42,989 --> 00:03:44,457
Is it gonna be a big wedding?

56
00:03:44,624 --> 00:03:47,651
Between the both of us, we know
just about everybody in Mt. Harlan,

57
00:03:47,828 --> 00:03:50,764
so we had to invite everyone
so no one would feel left out.

58
00:03:51,531 --> 00:03:54,933
It won't be much of a reception,
I'm afraid. It's not in the budget.

59
00:03:55,936 --> 00:03:57,598
Well, we can certainly chip in.

60
00:03:57,771 --> 00:03:58,795
No. No, no.

61
00:03:58,972 --> 00:04:01,012
- I didn't come looking for handouts.
- No handouts.

62
00:04:01,141 --> 00:04:03,621
We could host an open house
the day before the wedding.

63
00:04:03,777 --> 00:04:06,008
- No, no handouts.
- Dad, it's a wedding.

64
00:04:06,246 --> 00:04:07,805
The wedding is the event.

65
00:04:07,981 --> 00:04:10,421
We'll have it here before
the rehearsal at the church,

66
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:12,610
sort of a pre-wedding reception.

67
00:04:12,786 --> 00:04:14,584
- Oh, that'd be great.
- Oh!

68
00:04:14,755 --> 00:04:16,417
Thank you so much.

69
00:04:16,590 --> 00:04:19,116
Thank you. Well, I have to
be going. I'll see my way out.

70
00:04:19,292 --> 00:04:21,812
- Thank you both. Okay, thank you.
- Okay, we'll talk soon. Bye.

71
00:04:21,962 --> 00:04:25,763
Oh, Dad, a wedding reception
right here at the house.

72
00:04:26,366 --> 00:04:29,165
Doesn't that make
you feel fabulous?

73
00:04:29,402 --> 00:04:31,769
Oh, Dad.

74
00:05:01,501 --> 00:05:02,764
Hi.

75
00:05:02,969 --> 00:05:06,133
How are you? Hi.

76
00:05:09,009 --> 00:05:10,875
- Hi. Chips?
- Thanks!

77
00:05:11,044 --> 00:05:13,673
And then the little guy says,
"You better watch yourself, bud."

78
00:05:24,191 --> 00:05:25,750
Where did these
people come from?

79
00:05:25,926 --> 00:05:27,087
Not Mars.

80
00:05:29,162 --> 00:05:32,291
Ben, Ben, Ben, excuse me, Ben.
Can I talk to you for one second?

81
00:05:32,465 --> 00:05:33,797
- One second?
- I gotta get out.

82
00:05:33,967 --> 00:05:37,335
Practically the whole of
Mt. Harlan's here. Ha, ha.

83
00:05:37,504 --> 00:05:38,836
You think Chuck will come?

84
00:05:39,005 --> 00:05:41,065
Chuck? Yeah, why not?

85
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:43,403
In spite of what happened,
he's still Laura's brother.

86
00:05:43,577 --> 00:05:45,569
Come on. Let's mingle. Ha, ha.

87
00:05:49,015 --> 00:05:50,381
Let's go. MAN: Let's go watch.

88
00:05:50,851 --> 00:05:52,149
Keep going, keep going!

89
00:06:04,531 --> 00:06:05,760
Come on, man.

90
00:06:15,642 --> 00:06:18,771
Time! Time! Gray has got it.

91
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:22,740
The winner and still champ.

92
00:06:22,916 --> 00:06:25,078
So, what else you think
you can beat me at, Matt?

93
00:06:25,852 --> 00:06:29,186
- You cheated.
- I cheated. How could I cheat?

94
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:31,888
You didn't sit up all the way.

95
00:06:32,058 --> 00:06:35,256
I kicked your butt fair and
square just like I always do.

96
00:06:35,428 --> 00:06:38,159
He hasn't won a sports event
since he played high school football.

97
00:06:38,331 --> 00:06:41,358
The only reason he won was because
he was on a championship team.

98
00:06:41,534 --> 00:06:44,060
- Ooh.
- Yeah, keep it up, Brad.

99
00:06:44,237 --> 00:06:46,001
It'll make you look
all the more stupid

100
00:06:46,172 --> 00:06:48,607
when I win the volunteer
fireman's competition this year.

101
00:06:48,775 --> 00:06:51,768
- That's a laugh.
- No. That is a fact.

102
00:06:51,945 --> 00:06:55,473
I'm gonna kick your butt then
too. It gets easier every year.

103
00:06:55,649 --> 00:06:57,117
You want to know why, Matthew?

104
00:06:57,284 --> 00:07:00,152
Because every year, that butt of
yours gets just a little bit bigger.

105
00:07:06,593 --> 00:07:08,789
- How about a sandwich?
- Oh, thanks.

106
00:07:11,665 --> 00:07:13,133
Thanks.

107
00:07:16,002 --> 00:07:18,471
When does Obermeir get here?

108
00:07:19,205 --> 00:07:20,639
When's she leaving?

109
00:07:20,807 --> 00:07:22,901
- Where have you been?
- Outside.

110
00:07:23,076 --> 00:07:26,205
Thanks! Now, that's more like it.
Those chips were kind of greasy.

111
00:07:30,951 --> 00:07:32,579
Excuse me, Pop.

112
00:07:32,752 --> 00:07:35,415
Ben. Ben. Ben!

113
00:07:35,588 --> 00:07:39,753
Come here. I want you
to meet Ryland Hayward.

114
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:41,394
- George Hayward's boy?
- That's me.

115
00:07:41,561 --> 00:07:45,225
- This is my girlfriend, Sarah Eldridge.
- Hi, it's nice to meet you.

116
00:07:45,398 --> 00:07:47,026
Ry, I really need some water.

117
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:50,534
You come with me. I'll
find you something to drink.

118
00:07:51,037 --> 00:07:54,439
Poor girl's practically
dying of thirst, Ben.

119
00:07:54,607 --> 00:07:56,075
You're not a very good host.

120
00:07:59,145 --> 00:08:02,775
This guy is really something, isn't he,
Mr. Matlock? Always on top of things.

121
00:08:03,383 --> 00:08:07,115
Working with one of the most
famous lawyers in the country.

122
00:08:07,287 --> 00:08:11,383
Writing briefs, arguing before
the Supreme Court. Heh.

123
00:08:14,127 --> 00:08:16,323
It's a little stuffy in here.
Why don't we go outside?

124
00:08:16,496 --> 00:08:20,866
- Excuse us, Ry. WOMAN:
He said that? Really? Oh.

125
00:08:21,067 --> 00:08:24,094
- Oh. God, there's too many people.
- Yeah.

126
00:08:24,270 --> 00:08:27,331
- I need to talk to you about something.
- I got to go to the store.

127
00:08:27,841 --> 00:08:29,776
Make a list. Give me
something to write with.

128
00:08:29,943 --> 00:08:33,436
Okay. It's kind of important. I've been
wanting to mention it for some time.

129
00:08:33,613 --> 00:08:37,175
- Bologna. Everybody likes bologna.
- Bologna, speaking of bologna, um...

130
00:08:37,350 --> 00:08:38,477
Family pack.

131
00:08:38,651 --> 00:08:40,451
I may have overstated
my current employment...

132
00:08:40,587 --> 00:08:42,907
- My current employment status.
- Maybe four or five packs.

133
00:08:43,056 --> 00:08:46,083
- Hot dogs. A couple dozen packs.
- Some people I went to school with

134
00:08:46,259 --> 00:08:47,989
- back at Mt. Harlan...
- Buns.

135
00:08:48,194 --> 00:08:50,686
- Ketchup, mustard.
- I don't want to let anyone down.

136
00:08:50,864 --> 00:08:54,995
I wanted people to know that even if
somebody comes from a small town,

137
00:08:55,168 --> 00:08:57,330
they can make
something of themselves.

138
00:08:57,504 --> 00:09:01,464
I more or less told people that I
was a full partner in your law firm.

139
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:08,810
I didn't really lie.

140
00:09:09,416 --> 00:09:14,013
I didn't mean to lie. I just... One
slight misrepresentation of the facts

141
00:09:14,754 --> 00:09:17,986
led to another and things
just sort of snowballed.

142
00:09:19,692 --> 00:09:23,151
I don't have a partner.
Leanne ain't even a partner.

143
00:09:23,329 --> 00:09:28,529
I know, and I'm sorry. And it would
never, ever happen again. Ever.

144
00:09:29,102 --> 00:09:34,063
But since it has, and since it would
be embarrassing to tell everyone I lied,

145
00:09:35,108 --> 00:09:39,603
couldn't you just go along with
me for one day, two days, maybe,

146
00:09:39,779 --> 00:09:42,749
until everyone goes back
to Mt. Harlan? Please?

147
00:09:45,085 --> 00:09:46,109
No.

148
00:09:55,161 --> 00:09:57,289
Every time I come in
here, there's more people.

149
00:09:57,464 --> 00:09:59,695
They're like rabbits,
they just eat and multiply.

150
00:09:59,866 --> 00:10:02,335
Well, let's remember Laura
and Paul are having a good time,

151
00:10:02,502 --> 00:10:05,267
- and that's all that really matters.
- All right, cookies.

152
00:10:06,473 --> 00:10:09,807
You listen to me and you listen
good. You don't take the whole platter.

153
00:10:09,976 --> 00:10:13,913
You take one cookie. You
got that? One cookie. One.

154
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:15,605
Yes, ma'am.

155
00:10:23,723 --> 00:10:24,884
Hello, Ben.

156
00:10:25,458 --> 00:10:27,518
Nice party. Must have
been Leanne's idea.

157
00:10:27,694 --> 00:10:29,174
Where's he going
with those suitcases?

158
00:10:29,329 --> 00:10:31,764
Him and a couple other
people couldn't find motel rooms,

159
00:10:31,931 --> 00:10:34,332
- so I told them they could stay here.
- You what?

160
00:10:34,501 --> 00:10:37,198
I got people staying
at my house too, Ben.

161
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:41,774
You run out of beds upstairs, let
them sleep down here on the floor.

162
00:10:41,941 --> 00:10:45,605
Upstairs. Boys to the
right, girls to the left.

163
00:10:47,180 --> 00:10:51,174
Everybody? Everybody who's in the
wedding needs to get to the church.

164
00:10:51,351 --> 00:10:52,910
It's time for the rehearsal.

165
00:11:00,527 --> 00:11:04,555
Not too fast. Slow
down. Flowers high.

166
00:11:05,331 --> 00:11:06,822
That's it.

167
00:11:11,171 --> 00:11:12,935
This is killing me.

168
00:11:14,641 --> 00:11:16,405
We've got to talk.

169
00:11:23,349 --> 00:11:28,447
All right, Laura. As soon as your
maid of honor turns around, you start.

170
00:11:33,526 --> 00:11:37,054
Last time I did this, it
didn't turn out too good.

171
00:11:37,463 --> 00:11:39,227
- Leanne's divorced?
- Yeah.

172
00:11:39,399 --> 00:11:41,630
Married outside her species.

173
00:11:42,535 --> 00:11:44,766
He was a jackass.

174
00:11:53,346 --> 00:11:54,814
All right. That's very nice.

175
00:11:55,815 --> 00:11:58,751
How much you gonna rake
in this year? A hundred grand?

176
00:11:58,918 --> 00:12:02,184
Yeah, probably.
Probably about that much.

177
00:12:02,355 --> 00:12:06,656
Why are you living in that dumpy place
instead of some nice, big ritzy condo?

178
00:12:06,826 --> 00:12:08,795
Yeah. Sarah and I
could be staying with you

179
00:12:08,962 --> 00:12:12,160
instead of with those two escapees
from <i>The Love Boat</i> up there.

180
00:12:12,565 --> 00:12:16,798
You know, I haven't found a place,
um, you know, ritzy enough yet.

181
00:12:16,970 --> 00:12:19,770
Speaking of <i>The Love Boat</i>, when
are you and Sarah going to get married?

182
00:12:19,939 --> 00:12:21,908
Isn't it about time
you fished or cut bait?

183
00:12:22,075 --> 00:12:24,909
Well, my job still keeps
me on the road a lot.

184
00:12:25,078 --> 00:12:28,879
I figure it's kind of stupid to get married
if you're not home to enjoy the perks.

185
00:12:29,315 --> 00:12:30,339
Oh.

186
00:12:30,516 --> 00:12:34,180
So who is going to Paul's
bachelor party tonight?

187
00:12:35,021 --> 00:12:36,649
The real question is,

188
00:12:36,823 --> 00:12:40,590
who's going to be able to drive
home from Paul's bachelor party?

189
00:12:41,961 --> 00:12:45,454
Ha, ha. Shh. Ahem.

190
00:13:04,250 --> 00:13:06,210
- You got it, Matt.
- Come on now, Matt.

191
00:13:06,352 --> 00:13:08,014
- Go, go, go!
- Push it!

192
00:13:12,125 --> 00:13:13,593
I'm gonna whoop
you so bad this year,

193
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:17,390
- you'll be lucky to win the cake walk.
- Two out of three. Come on.

194
00:13:17,930 --> 00:13:18,989
All right!

195
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:25,710
You'd think they would've
outgrown this by now.

196
00:13:25,872 --> 00:13:28,671
Yeah, well, once a
jock, always a jock.

197
00:13:36,149 --> 00:13:37,515
Chuck!

198
00:13:41,521 --> 00:13:42,921
Glad you could make it.

199
00:13:43,089 --> 00:13:45,786
You all remember Laura's
brother, Chuck, don't you?

200
00:13:48,294 --> 00:13:50,923
- I remember him.
- Hey, hey. Brad, don't start.

201
00:13:56,102 --> 00:13:58,037
Come on. You
don't have to leave.

202
00:13:58,204 --> 00:14:01,538
Oh, yes, I do. Have
a beer. Enjoy yourself.

203
00:14:03,576 --> 00:14:05,977
- I don't drink anymore.
- Oh, well, aren't you the saint?

204
00:14:06,145 --> 00:14:08,046
- Come on, Brad.
- Too bad it's a little late.

205
00:14:08,314 --> 00:14:10,943
You were drinking the night you
drove my brother into that tree.

206
00:14:11,117 --> 00:14:13,279
- Take it easy, Brad.
- Have you seen him lately?

207
00:14:14,187 --> 00:14:15,450
Here, I'll show you a picture.

208
00:14:15,621 --> 00:14:17,817
This is him at Christmas in
his brand-new wheelchair.

209
00:14:18,458 --> 00:14:20,791
I'm sorry, Brad. It
was an accident.

210
00:14:21,627 --> 00:14:24,563
- One I'll regret for the rest of my life.
- You bet you will.

211
00:14:40,747 --> 00:14:42,707
I'm sorry, man, I didn't
mean to wreck your party.

212
00:14:42,849 --> 00:14:45,182
- I know.
- I mean, I tried to leave.

213
00:14:45,351 --> 00:14:50,847
I know. Look, I left the vows in the
chapel. Sit down. Sober up, okay?

214
00:14:51,023 --> 00:14:52,423
- Okay.
- Good.

215
00:15:18,317 --> 00:15:23,278
Found them. Looks like
the wedding's on... again.

216
00:16:23,983 --> 00:16:26,782
Morning, Mr. Matlock. I
made coffee. Hope that's okay.

217
00:16:26,953 --> 00:16:28,581
Any left?

218
00:16:33,493 --> 00:16:34,859
That smells good!

219
00:16:35,361 --> 00:16:37,626
Morning, Ben. Oh,
good, you made coffee.

220
00:16:37,797 --> 00:16:40,096
Uh, cups are right over
there. Help yourself.

221
00:16:41,267 --> 00:16:42,701
Oh, good.

222
00:16:46,272 --> 00:16:47,968
What are you doing here?

223
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:50,473
I brought my house
guests over for breakfast.

224
00:16:50,643 --> 00:16:53,112
Why can't they
eat at your house?

225
00:16:53,279 --> 00:16:57,740
I'm a retired farmer, Ben. I can't
afford to feed all these people.

226
00:17:01,687 --> 00:17:05,180
What happened to all the
food? This thing was full.

227
00:17:05,358 --> 00:17:08,692
You're not a very good
host. Better go to the market.

228
00:17:08,861 --> 00:17:12,559
Dad. Dad. Dad.

229
00:17:12,732 --> 00:17:15,998
I just came from Laura's place.
Something horrible has happened.

230
00:17:21,774 --> 00:17:25,302
He punched me, and
then I tried to punch him,

231
00:17:25,478 --> 00:17:27,379
and then we just went at it.

232
00:17:29,015 --> 00:17:31,075
I don't know. Then I
turned around and left.

233
00:17:32,451 --> 00:17:37,890
What is supposed to be a wonderful
event has just turned into a nightmare.

234
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:41,493
When you left, where'd you go?

235
00:17:42,328 --> 00:17:46,060
I went to Laura's. I turned the
TV on. I fell asleep on the couch.

236
00:17:46,232 --> 00:17:48,258
She there? CHUCK: No.

237
00:17:48,434 --> 00:17:51,563
Her and the bridesmaids were
out someplace. Next thing I know,

238
00:17:51,737 --> 00:17:54,571
she was waking me up, telling me
there's cops wanted to talk to me.

239
00:17:56,142 --> 00:17:58,805
I knew Brad was
gonna be at the party.

240
00:18:00,112 --> 00:18:03,947
I just wanted to see if he'd
forgive me for his brother.

241
00:18:04,684 --> 00:18:06,050
His brother?

242
00:18:06,218 --> 00:18:09,279
Yeah, Frank, he
was a football player.

243
00:18:09,455 --> 00:18:12,323
Pro teams were starting to scout
him when he was in this car accident.

244
00:18:12,491 --> 00:18:16,656
He was paralyzed from the waist
down. Chuck here was driving.

245
00:18:16,829 --> 00:18:18,457
I was drunk.

246
00:18:18,798 --> 00:18:20,733
Brad hated my guts.

247
00:18:21,067 --> 00:18:22,695
I can't say as I blame him.

248
00:18:23,336 --> 00:18:25,271
People in town get upset?

249
00:18:27,139 --> 00:18:28,334
Are you kidding?

250
00:18:29,108 --> 00:18:32,510
Frank was like Mt. Harlan's
greatest football player ever.

251
00:18:33,412 --> 00:18:35,881
It was as if I injured
him on purpose.

252
00:18:36,983 --> 00:18:38,747
I was drunk.

253
00:18:39,619 --> 00:18:40,985
It was an accident.

254
00:18:41,153 --> 00:18:44,351
Well, the police found
the knife that killed Brad

255
00:18:44,523 --> 00:18:46,082
in back of Laura's house.

256
00:18:46,258 --> 00:18:47,317
Look...

257
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:51,096
Mr. Matlock, I
didn't put it there.

258
00:18:55,735 --> 00:18:59,638
I did not kill Brad. I swear.

259
00:19:03,809 --> 00:19:05,505
I'll see if I can get
you out of here.

260
00:19:06,212 --> 00:19:08,511
Thanks. Both of you.

261
00:19:11,283 --> 00:19:13,149
I sure am glad you
two are partners.

262
00:19:34,006 --> 00:19:36,271
- Hi.
- Hi.

263
00:19:38,511 --> 00:19:40,503
It's Sarah, right?

264
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:42,114
Sarah Eldridge.

265
00:19:42,281 --> 00:19:44,978
- Ry's girlfriend?
- Yeah.

266
00:19:47,353 --> 00:19:48,616
You okay?

267
00:19:48,788 --> 00:19:51,019
Actually, my
stomach's a little upset.

268
00:19:51,190 --> 00:19:53,284
Are there any crackers
or something around here?

269
00:19:53,459 --> 00:19:54,893
Sure, I just bought some.

270
00:19:55,061 --> 00:19:57,587
If you wait about five minutes, I
can make some scrambled eggs.

271
00:19:57,763 --> 00:20:00,597
Oh, no, I'll be lucky if I
can keep these down.

272
00:20:00,766 --> 00:20:02,928
Too much to drink last night?

273
00:20:03,269 --> 00:20:05,602
That and what happened to Brad.

274
00:20:08,207 --> 00:20:09,231
Did you know him well?

275
00:20:10,543 --> 00:20:12,273
He was a friend.

276
00:20:12,445 --> 00:20:13,879
I'm sorry.

277
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:16,278
Thanks for the crackers.

278
00:20:16,449 --> 00:20:17,747
Sure.

279
00:20:27,693 --> 00:20:29,594
His brother-in-law
is a policeman.

280
00:20:31,997 --> 00:20:33,488
Have you seen Chuck?

281
00:20:33,666 --> 00:20:35,897
Yeah. He'll be out
on bail tomorrow.

282
00:20:36,068 --> 00:20:38,060
Man, how could something
so awful have happened?

283
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:40,536
- I don't know.
- We've postponed the wedding.

284
00:20:40,973 --> 00:20:42,999
Come on, sweetheart.
You need to eat something.

285
00:20:43,175 --> 00:20:44,905
Mm-hm. Mm-hm.

286
00:20:46,011 --> 00:20:50,415
So, uh, Mr. Matlock, did you
come up with a game plan yet?

287
00:20:51,183 --> 00:20:53,618
- Game plan?
- Yeah, for defending Chuck.

288
00:20:53,786 --> 00:20:57,780
Cliff told me that's the first thing you
always do, come up with a game plan.

289
00:20:58,290 --> 00:21:00,759
Not yet. Excuse me.

290
00:21:04,230 --> 00:21:05,425
- No she isn't.
- She is.

291
00:21:05,598 --> 00:21:06,793
- She can't be.
- Why not?

292
00:21:06,966 --> 00:21:08,594
Because Ry would have told me.

293
00:21:08,768 --> 00:21:10,464
- Told you what?
- That Sarah's pregnant.

294
00:21:10,636 --> 00:21:12,696
She is not... She has an
upset stomach, no big deal.

295
00:21:12,872 --> 00:21:16,138
She said she had a hangover but
Laura said she didn't drink last night,

296
00:21:16,308 --> 00:21:18,228
which was another
indication that she's pregnant.

297
00:21:18,377 --> 00:21:21,177
Ry Hayward is not the type who
would keep something like this a secret.

298
00:21:21,347 --> 00:21:23,509
- Maybe he didn't know about it.
- Well...

299
00:21:23,682 --> 00:21:26,618
- Why wouldn't she tell him?
- Good question.

300
00:21:26,786 --> 00:21:31,815
You done? Because if you
are, Cliff and I need to talk.

301
00:21:32,591 --> 00:21:33,786
I'd really like to,

302
00:21:33,959 --> 00:21:37,327
uh, but Detective Garrison
said he would meet me, uh,

303
00:21:37,496 --> 00:21:41,866
at the hotel where Brad was
staying, so I should really go do that.

304
00:21:42,034 --> 00:21:44,128
Sorry. See you later. Bye.

305
00:21:54,346 --> 00:21:56,975
That's what we always talked
about when we were kids,

306
00:21:58,017 --> 00:22:01,181
how we were all gonna grow
up and become volunteer firemen.

307
00:22:01,353 --> 00:22:03,015
So why didn't you?

308
00:22:03,556 --> 00:22:06,185
Huh. Became a lawyer.

309
00:22:06,358 --> 00:22:09,624
I guess if I hadn't, I'd be
putting out fires right now.

310
00:22:11,997 --> 00:22:15,729
Oh, we, uh, found a list of names
and phone numbers in his wallet.

311
00:22:15,901 --> 00:22:18,632
- Oh? Did you check it out?
- Some of them.

312
00:22:18,804 --> 00:22:20,670
He was hitting up
people for money.

313
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:23,071
Really? Say what
he needed it for?

314
00:22:23,242 --> 00:22:24,835
Said his car broke down.

315
00:22:25,010 --> 00:22:27,206
Oh, and, uh, see
this last number here?

316
00:22:27,379 --> 00:22:29,109
It's a doctor's office.

317
00:22:29,281 --> 00:22:31,079
Gynecologist.

318
00:22:47,333 --> 00:22:48,801
How's your stomach feeling?

319
00:22:48,968 --> 00:22:50,163
So-so.

320
00:22:50,336 --> 00:22:52,601
How far along are you?

321
00:22:55,207 --> 00:22:57,540
What makes you
think I'm pregnant?

322
00:22:57,710 --> 00:23:01,078
Enough of my friends have had
babies for me to know the signs.

323
00:23:04,550 --> 00:23:06,212
How come you haven't told Ry?

324
00:23:07,653 --> 00:23:11,454
Maybe you're wrong.
Maybe there's nothing to tell.

325
00:23:11,957 --> 00:23:14,017
I spoke to Cliff.

326
00:23:14,193 --> 00:23:17,823
He said Brad was asking
everybody he knew here in Atlanta

327
00:23:17,997 --> 00:23:19,465
if he could borrow money.

328
00:23:19,632 --> 00:23:21,828
Seems he needed
about a thousand dollars.

329
00:23:22,001 --> 00:23:27,167
Made an appointment with an
OB/GYN for someone named Sally Eller.

330
00:23:27,339 --> 00:23:29,331
That's you, isn't it?

331
00:23:31,343 --> 00:23:33,574
Is the baby Brad's?

332
00:23:35,414 --> 00:23:37,110
Brad was murdered, Sarah.

333
00:23:37,283 --> 00:23:40,310
Questions aren't going
to stop here, believe me.

334
00:23:48,794 --> 00:23:51,286
I can never get everything
back in this thing.

335
00:23:53,499 --> 00:23:55,263
- Ready to roll?
- Yeah.

336
00:23:56,201 --> 00:23:58,898
- Is there something the matter?
- Just take me home.

337
00:23:59,071 --> 00:24:01,506
Okay. Let's go say
goodbye to Laura.

338
00:24:01,674 --> 00:24:05,668
Now, be sure and look
under all the furniture.

339
00:24:05,844 --> 00:24:09,713
I wouldn't want you to come back
here just to pick up a toothbrush. Heh.

340
00:24:09,882 --> 00:24:11,214
Uh, Ben.

341
00:24:11,383 --> 00:24:13,375
Uh, excuse me, do you
remember Detective Garrison?

342
00:24:13,585 --> 00:24:15,144
Oh, yeah, how are you?

343
00:24:15,321 --> 00:24:17,517
- These people came for the wedding?
- Yeah.

344
00:24:17,690 --> 00:24:19,852
- What are they doing?
- They're leaving.

345
00:24:20,025 --> 00:24:23,154
They can't do that. Hey,
hey! Hold on a minute.

346
00:24:24,196 --> 00:24:26,631
Detective Robert Garrison,
Atlanta Police Department.

347
00:24:26,799 --> 00:24:29,598
I'm investigating the
murder of Bradley Thorne.

348
00:24:29,768 --> 00:24:33,261
I'm afraid you'll have to stay right
here until my people ask some questions.

349
00:24:34,473 --> 00:24:37,773
Uh, shouldn't take long,
just, uh, two or three days.

350
00:24:37,943 --> 00:24:39,036
Four at the most.

351
00:24:53,625 --> 00:24:55,093
Thanks, Deb.

352
00:25:00,399 --> 00:25:02,027
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

353
00:25:10,809 --> 00:25:13,210
It feels good to get away
from all those people.

354
00:25:13,645 --> 00:25:15,113
Yeah.

355
00:25:15,381 --> 00:25:16,679
Sure does.

356
00:25:19,485 --> 00:25:20,851
How'd it go with the cops?

357
00:25:21,754 --> 00:25:24,349
They asked me what
I was doing that night

358
00:25:24,656 --> 00:25:26,456
and I told them I was
partying with the girls.

359
00:25:31,663 --> 00:25:35,691
- I can't drink this all. Have some beer.
- No, thanks.

360
00:25:35,868 --> 00:25:37,769
Just one glass.

361
00:25:37,936 --> 00:25:39,598
I said no.

362
00:25:40,272 --> 00:25:41,900
You know,

363
00:25:42,074 --> 00:25:44,543
heh, you used to
drink beer all the time.

364
00:25:44,710 --> 00:25:46,611
Now you never drink it at all.

365
00:25:46,912 --> 00:25:49,211
In fact, you never drink
any alcohol anymore.

366
00:25:49,381 --> 00:25:52,442
- So maybe I don't want to.
- Why not?

367
00:25:56,021 --> 00:25:58,889
I just want to know what's
going on with you, Sarah.

368
00:25:59,058 --> 00:26:00,856
Tell me.

369
00:26:01,026 --> 00:26:02,119
Nothing.

370
00:26:05,898 --> 00:26:09,494
Damn it, how can you even think
of having that kid? Brad is dead!

371
00:26:20,279 --> 00:26:23,977
I can count the days
as good as you can.

372
00:26:24,550 --> 00:26:28,612
- And you never said anything.
- What could I say?

373
00:26:30,622 --> 00:26:34,787
I was gone for a whole month,
Sarah. I knew it wasn't mine.

374
00:26:35,360 --> 00:26:37,386
How did you know it was Brad's?

375
00:26:39,731 --> 00:26:42,667
I saw the way he stared at you,

376
00:26:42,835 --> 00:26:46,294
I saw how you looked at
him. This was no secret.

377
00:26:48,640 --> 00:26:51,474
Don't do this to me, please.

378
00:26:51,677 --> 00:26:54,169
Don't do this to us.

379
00:26:57,116 --> 00:26:59,415
Where were you that night, Ry?

380
00:27:02,621 --> 00:27:06,251
- What night?
- The night Brad was murdered.

381
00:27:06,425 --> 00:27:09,520
You didn't get into bed till after
2. He was murdered around 1.

382
00:27:09,695 --> 00:27:12,221
I don't believe this.

383
00:27:13,565 --> 00:27:16,034
You think I had something
to do with Brad's death?

384
00:27:16,201 --> 00:27:17,965
You weren't at Laura's.

385
00:27:20,139 --> 00:27:24,304
It took me a while to drive back
from the bachelor party. I got lost.

386
00:27:24,476 --> 00:27:27,241
Why didn't you go home with Cliff
like you said you were going to?

387
00:27:29,381 --> 00:27:32,044
Look. Here are the keys.

388
00:27:32,251 --> 00:27:34,686
I'll walk back to Laura's.

389
00:28:30,209 --> 00:28:33,441
Morning, Ben. Want some coffee?
- Yeah.

390
00:28:33,612 --> 00:28:36,912
You'll have to make another
pot. We finished this one.

391
00:28:53,565 --> 00:28:55,727
What's for breakfast?

392
00:28:56,268 --> 00:28:58,066
It's in the fridge.

393
00:29:03,809 --> 00:29:05,175
Where?

394
00:29:05,711 --> 00:29:07,805
Bottom shelf.

395
00:29:09,114 --> 00:29:11,549
You'll have to do
better than this.

396
00:29:12,384 --> 00:29:15,286
Are you blind, or what?

397
00:29:16,288 --> 00:29:21,522
There were four dozen jelly
doughnuts in there just last night.

398
00:29:21,693 --> 00:29:25,824
That explains all those little
red stains on your sofa in there.

399
00:29:25,998 --> 00:29:28,263
I thought there was
another murder. Heh.

400
00:29:30,669 --> 00:29:33,571
Hey. Where can I find
some clean towels?

401
00:29:49,288 --> 00:29:52,725
Ooh. What are these?

402
00:29:53,258 --> 00:29:58,629
Those are pictures some fella
took at Paul's bachelor party.

403
00:29:59,364 --> 00:30:01,697
- How about getting me a set?
- What for?

404
00:30:02,834 --> 00:30:04,894
What do you think?

405
00:30:06,138 --> 00:30:09,006
- Whew.
- I might need them.

406
00:30:09,174 --> 00:30:14,272
I know who killed Brad. He won the Mt.
Harlan Volunteer Fireman Competition

407
00:30:14,446 --> 00:30:15,709
for four straight years.

408
00:30:15,881 --> 00:30:18,680
I don't understand why
people get so excited

409
00:30:18,850 --> 00:30:21,945
about climbing ladders
and dragging hoses around.

410
00:30:22,120 --> 00:30:24,521
Yeah, well, to some people,
it's the high point of the year.

411
00:30:24,690 --> 00:30:26,386
You know how
it is in Mt. Harlan.

412
00:30:27,426 --> 00:30:33,332
Anyway, the fella who came in second
four straight years was Matt Brown.

413
00:30:33,498 --> 00:30:36,366
The fella that was just in
here looking for clean towels?

414
00:30:36,535 --> 00:30:40,165
Yeah. Cliff said they were always
competing against one another.

415
00:30:40,339 --> 00:30:46,108
Brad spent all his time at the bachelor
party beating Matt at hand wrestling.

416
00:30:46,912 --> 00:30:50,349
Cliff said he's an even worse
loser now than he was in high school,

417
00:30:50,515 --> 00:30:52,780
and in high school,
he was terrible.

418
00:30:56,488 --> 00:31:00,152
Ben, use your brain. Do I have
to spell everything out for you?

419
00:31:01,526 --> 00:31:04,519
You're saying Matt killed Brad
because he beat him at arm wrestling?

420
00:31:04,896 --> 00:31:06,159
No.

421
00:31:06,331 --> 00:31:08,095
He killed him because
he wanted to be sure

422
00:31:08,266 --> 00:31:10,826
he wins the fireman
competition this year.

423
00:31:13,372 --> 00:31:15,705
Stranger things have
happened, you know.

424
00:31:17,109 --> 00:31:19,203
I'm going to the store.

425
00:31:24,049 --> 00:31:26,575
Uh, you're out of toilet paper.

426
00:31:54,079 --> 00:31:55,843
Excuse me.

427
00:31:57,048 --> 00:31:58,710
- Hi.
- Sir.

428
00:31:58,917 --> 00:32:03,548
Uh, listen, I left something at the desk
for Brad Thorne a couple nights ago.

429
00:32:03,989 --> 00:32:06,686
- Brad Thorne is dead.
- Yeah, I know.

430
00:32:06,858 --> 00:32:10,556
And since he obviously never
got what I left for him, I'd like it back.

431
00:32:10,729 --> 00:32:12,391
It was in a white envelope.

432
00:32:12,864 --> 00:32:16,733
Okay, uh, perhaps it got put in
a file somewhere. I'll go check.

433
00:32:37,055 --> 00:32:38,853
Are you still back there?

434
00:32:39,024 --> 00:32:41,152
Yep. Still looking.

435
00:32:48,967 --> 00:32:52,961
Detective Robert Garrison, Atlanta
police. I'd like to ask some questions.

436
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:57,441
- About what?
- The thousand dollars you left here

437
00:32:57,609 --> 00:32:59,669
for Mr. Thorne the
night he was murdered.

438
00:33:37,249 --> 00:33:38,547
Hey, Mr. Matlock.

439
00:33:38,717 --> 00:33:40,276
- You need some help?
- Yeah, boy.

440
00:33:42,487 --> 00:33:45,582
- You go for a run?
- Yeah, I did five miles.

441
00:33:45,757 --> 00:33:48,056
I've been training for
the fireman competition.

442
00:33:49,427 --> 00:33:51,328
Something I got to ask you.

443
00:33:51,830 --> 00:33:53,025
Yeah, what's that?

444
00:33:55,100 --> 00:33:56,898
Well...

445
00:34:02,073 --> 00:34:03,541
Never mind.

446
00:34:03,708 --> 00:34:05,142
Forget it.

447
00:34:13,852 --> 00:34:16,879
- How you doing?
- Hey, Cliff, thanks for coming down.

448
00:34:17,455 --> 00:34:19,651
What are friends for?

449
00:34:26,398 --> 00:34:27,798
So,

450
00:34:28,700 --> 00:34:30,134
tell me about the money.

451
00:34:31,403 --> 00:34:33,838
Well, like I told the cops,

452
00:34:34,005 --> 00:34:36,474
Brad had lent me some money,
and I was just paying him back.

453
00:34:39,277 --> 00:34:41,576
The police think Brad
was blackmailing you.

454
00:34:42,914 --> 00:34:44,780
He wasn't blackmailing me.

455
00:34:45,417 --> 00:34:47,352
Well, he apparently
needed money badly.

456
00:34:47,519 --> 00:34:51,217
He called everyone he knew, trying
to scrape together a thousand dollars.

457
00:34:53,658 --> 00:34:56,492
I had a very long talk with
Sarah this morning, Ry.

458
00:34:58,129 --> 00:34:59,961
A very long talk.

459
00:35:06,438 --> 00:35:10,899
When I heard through a mutual
friend that Brad needed money,

460
00:35:11,142 --> 00:35:13,441
I put two and two
together and figured out

461
00:35:13,612 --> 00:35:16,844
that he needed it to
pay for an abortion.

462
00:35:17,082 --> 00:35:20,644
So I got the money

463
00:35:20,819 --> 00:35:24,620
and I left it for him at the
motel in a plain white envelope

464
00:35:24,789 --> 00:35:26,417
so he wouldn't know
it came from me.

465
00:35:26,591 --> 00:35:30,494
- You never confronted him?
- I just wanted everything to go away.

466
00:35:32,764 --> 00:35:35,825
I thought if I didn't rock the boat,
Sarah and I could just start over,

467
00:35:36,001 --> 00:35:39,597
and I could eventually make her
forget this whole thing ever happened.

468
00:35:39,771 --> 00:35:42,104
Where'd you go after
the bachelor party?

469
00:35:43,708 --> 00:35:48,169
Well, I heard Brad say that he was
gonna go to the church with Paul,

470
00:35:48,346 --> 00:35:51,407
so I dropped the money
off at the motel and then I...

471
00:35:52,050 --> 00:35:54,281
I just drove around.

472
00:35:54,686 --> 00:35:57,679
I wasn't feeling real good
about myself at that point.

473
00:36:00,358 --> 00:36:03,419
Mr. Hayward, we're all
done here. You can go.

474
00:36:03,595 --> 00:36:05,587
Appreciate your cooperation.

475
00:36:06,765 --> 00:36:09,530
Does this mean I'm
not a suspect anymore?

476
00:36:09,701 --> 00:36:13,035
Why drop off money for Brad if
you were planning on killing him?

477
00:36:13,204 --> 00:36:14,536
It doesn't make any sense.

478
00:36:14,706 --> 00:36:16,306
- Thank you very much, detective.
- Sure.

479
00:36:16,474 --> 00:36:19,569
